WEBVTT

00:00.080 --> 00:04.920
Now that is like not 100% standard way and rules for each layer.

00:04.960 --> 00:10.880
What you have to do as a data architect, you have to define exactly what is the purpose of each layer.

00:10.920 --> 00:15.400
So now what we're going to do, we're going to make it clear what is the main purpose of each layer.

00:15.440 --> 00:20.440
So we say it can store raw and unprocessed data as it is from the sources.

00:20.440 --> 00:21.800
And why we are doing that.

00:21.800 --> 00:24.120
It is for traceability and debugging.

00:24.120 --> 00:28.680
If you have a layer where you are keeping the raw data, it is very important to have the data as it

00:28.680 --> 00:34.000
is from the sources, because we can go always back to the bronze layer and investigate the data of

00:34.000 --> 00:36.480
specific source if something goes wrong.

00:36.600 --> 00:43.520
So the main objective is to have raw, untouched data that can help you as a data engineer by analyzing

00:43.520 --> 00:44.760
the root cause of issues.

00:44.800 --> 00:46.440
Now moving on to the silver layer.

00:46.480 --> 00:50.400
It is the layer where we are going to store clean and standardized data.

00:50.400 --> 00:55.360
And this is the place where we are going to do basic transformations in order to prepare the data for

00:55.360 --> 00:56.440
the final layer.

00:56.480 --> 00:57.720
Now for the gold layer.

00:57.720 --> 00:59.910
It can contain business ready data.

00:59.950 --> 01:05.710
So the main goal here is to provide data that could be consumed by business users and analysts in order

01:05.710 --> 01:07.550
to build reporting and analytics.

01:07.590 --> 01:10.710
So with that, we have defined the main goal for each layer.

01:10.750 --> 01:14.270
Now next, what I would like to do is to define the object types.

01:14.310 --> 01:19.350
And since we are talking about a data warehouse and database, we have here generally two types either

01:19.390 --> 01:20.550
a table or a view.

01:20.590 --> 01:23.870
So we are going for the bronze layer and the silver layer with tables.

01:23.870 --> 01:26.350
But for the gold layer we are going with the views.

01:26.470 --> 01:31.990
So the best practice says for the last layer in your data warehouse make it virtual using views.

01:31.990 --> 01:37.030
It gives you a lot of dynamic and of course speed in order to build it, since we don't have to make

01:37.070 --> 01:38.110
a load process for it.

01:38.150 --> 01:41.270
And now the next step is that we're going to go and define the load methods.

01:41.270 --> 01:46.390
So in this project I have decided to go with the full load using the method of truncating and inserting.

01:46.390 --> 01:48.110
It is just faster and way easier.

01:48.110 --> 01:50.830
So we're going to say for the bronze layer we're going to go with the full load.

01:50.830 --> 01:53.510
And you have to specify as well for the silver layer as well.

01:53.510 --> 01:54.710
We're going to go with the full load.

01:54.710 --> 01:57.910
And of course for the views we don't need any load process.

01:57.910 --> 02:02.790
So each time you decide to go with tables, you have to define the load methods, whether full load,

02:02.790 --> 02:04.070
incremental loads and so on.

02:04.110 --> 02:07.310
Now we come to the very interesting part the data transformations.

02:07.350 --> 02:08.390
Now for the bronze layer.

02:08.390 --> 02:10.310
It is the easiest one about this topic.

02:10.310 --> 02:16.470
Because we don't have any transformations, we have to commit ourselves to not touch the data, do not

02:16.470 --> 02:18.310
manipulate it, don't change anything.

02:18.310 --> 02:19.950
So it's gonna stay as it is.

02:19.950 --> 02:22.590
If it comes apart, it's gonna stay bad in the bronze layer.

02:22.630 --> 02:25.350
And now we come to the silver layer where we have the heavy lifting.

02:25.390 --> 02:29.830
As we committed in the objective, we have to make clean and standardized data.

02:29.830 --> 02:32.470
And for that we have different types of transformations.

02:32.470 --> 02:37.150
So we have to do data cleansing, data standardizations data normalization.

02:37.150 --> 02:40.510
We have to go and derive new columns and data enrichments.

02:40.670 --> 02:45.430
So there are like a bunch of transformations that we have to do in order to prepare the data.

02:45.470 --> 02:51.150
Our focus here is to transform the data, to make it clean and following standards, and try to push

02:51.150 --> 02:53.670
all business transformations to the next layer.

02:53.710 --> 02:59.420
So that means in the gold layer we will be focusing on business transformations that is needed for the

02:59.420 --> 03:00.940
consumers for the use cases.

03:00.940 --> 03:04.340
So what we do here, we do data integrations between source system.

03:04.340 --> 03:06.060
We do that aggregations.

03:06.060 --> 03:08.580
We apply a lot of business logics and rules.

03:08.580 --> 03:13.020
And we build a data model that is ready for, for example, business intelligence.

03:13.020 --> 03:15.700
So here we do a lot of business transformations.

03:15.700 --> 03:18.780
And in the silver layer we do basic data transformations.

03:18.780 --> 03:22.940
So it is really here very important to make the fine decisions.

03:22.940 --> 03:25.820
What type of transformations to be done in each layer.

03:25.820 --> 03:28.300
And make sure that you commit to those rules.

03:28.340 --> 03:30.860
Now the next aspect is about the data modeling.

03:30.860 --> 03:32.860
In the bronze layer and the silver layer.

03:32.860 --> 03:36.060
We will not break the data model that comes from the source system.

03:36.060 --> 03:39.980
So if the source system deliver five tables, we can have here like five tables.

03:39.980 --> 03:45.740
And as well in the silver layer we will not go and denormalize or normalize or like make something new.

03:45.780 --> 03:48.500
We can leave it exactly like it comes from the source system.

03:48.500 --> 03:52.260
Because what we're going to do, we're going to build the data model in the gold layer.

03:52.260 --> 03:55.100
And here you have to define which data model you want to follow.

03:55.140 --> 03:57.770
Are you following the star schema, the snowflake?

03:57.770 --> 04:00.250
Or are you just making aggregated objects?

04:00.250 --> 04:04.890
So you have to go and make a list of all data models types that you can follow in the gold layer.

04:04.890 --> 04:08.410
And at the end, what you can specify in each layer is the target audience.

04:08.410 --> 04:11.170
And this is of course a very important decision in the bronze layer.

04:11.170 --> 04:13.650
You don't want to give access to any end user.

04:13.650 --> 04:18.090
It is really important to make sure that only data engineers access the bronze layer.

04:18.130 --> 04:24.610
It makes no sense for data analysts or data scientists to go to the bad data, because you have a better

04:24.610 --> 04:26.850
version for that in the silver layer.

04:26.850 --> 04:30.890
So in the silver layer, of course, the data engineers have to have an access to it, and as well the

04:30.890 --> 04:33.290
data analysts and the data scientists and so on.

04:33.290 --> 04:39.010
But still, you don't give it to any business user that can't deal with the raw data model from the

04:39.010 --> 04:43.090
sources, because for the business users, you're going to get a better layer for them.

04:43.090 --> 04:44.650
And that is the gold layer.

04:44.650 --> 04:50.490
So the gold layer, it is suitable for the data analyst and as well the business users, because usually

04:50.490 --> 04:55.120
the business users don't have a deep knowledge on the technicality of the silver server layer.

04:55.120 --> 05:00.160
So if you are designing multiple layers, you have to discuss all those topics and make a clear decision

05:00.160 --> 05:01.280
for each layer.

05:01.480 --> 05:02.320
All right my friends.

05:02.320 --> 05:07.800
So now before we proceed with the design, I want to tell you a secret principle concepts that each

05:07.840 --> 05:09.360
data architect must know.

05:09.360 --> 05:12.200
And that is the separation of concerns.

05:12.200 --> 05:13.200
So what is that?

05:13.200 --> 05:19.000
As you are designing an architecture, you have to make sure to break down the complex system into smaller

05:19.000 --> 05:20.360
independent parts.

05:20.360 --> 05:23.720
And each part is responsible for a specific task.

05:23.760 --> 05:25.160
And here comes the magic.

05:25.360 --> 05:28.760
The component of your architecture must not be duplicated.

05:28.760 --> 05:31.760
So you cannot have two parts are doing the same thing.

05:31.760 --> 05:34.600
So the idea here is to not mix everything.

05:34.600 --> 05:38.200
And this is one of the biggest mistakes in any big projects.

05:38.200 --> 05:40.480
And I have shown that almost everywhere.

05:40.480 --> 05:44.480
So a good data architects follow this concept, this principle.

05:44.480 --> 05:48.960
So for example, if you are looking to our data architecture, we have already done that.

05:48.960 --> 05:52.400
So we have defined a unique set of tasks for each layer.

05:52.400 --> 05:54.960
So for example we have set seeds in the silver layer.

05:54.960 --> 05:59.600
We do data cleansing, but in the gold layer we do business transformations.

05:59.600 --> 06:04.120
And with that you will not be allowed to do any business transformations in the silver layer.

06:04.120 --> 06:06.120
And the same thing goes for the gold layer.

06:06.160 --> 06:08.560
You don't do in the gold layer any data cleansing.

06:08.560 --> 06:11.640
So each layer has its own unique tasks.

06:11.640 --> 06:14.360
And the same thing goes for the bronze layer and the silver layer.

06:14.400 --> 06:20.040
You do not allow to load data from the source systems directly to the silver layer, because we have

06:20.040 --> 06:21.880
decided the landing layer.

06:21.920 --> 06:23.800
The first layer is the bronze layer.

06:23.840 --> 06:28.840
Otherwise you will have like a set of source systems that are loaded first to the bronze layer, and

06:28.840 --> 06:32.040
another set is skipping the layer and going to the silver.

06:32.040 --> 06:33.720
And with that we have overlapping.

06:33.720 --> 06:36.880
You are doing data ingestion in two different layers.

06:36.880 --> 06:42.120
So my friends, if you have this mindset separation of concerns, I promise you you're going to be a

06:42.120 --> 06:43.480
top data architect.

06:43.480 --> 06:44.600
So think about it.

06:44.600 --> 06:45.400
All right my friends.

06:45.400 --> 06:48.000
So with that we have designed the layers of the data warehouse.

06:48.000 --> 06:49.560
We can go ahead and close it.

06:49.560 --> 06:53.440
The next step we're going to go to Draw.io and start drawing the data architecture.
